---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO } from "../lib/release";

// Harbor's own service (service/collect in the repository; the app has the same address in HarborService).
const SERVICE = "https://liipwgofoskaybprarjz.supabase.co/functions/v1/collect";
---

<Base
  title="Harbor privacy: what the app sends, and when"
  description="Harbor sends nothing about you unless you say so. Every field of the optional statistics and of a problem report, where it is kept and for how long."
>
  <section class="banner">
    <div class="container banner__in">
      <div>
        <h1>What Harbor sends.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Nothing about you, unless you say so. No account, no advertising, and this website loads nothing from
          anyone else and counts no visitors.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container">
    <section class="release release--words" id="always">
      <div class="release__head"><h2>Without asking</h2></div>
      <ul>
        <li><span>Harbor looks for updates to itself and to its Windows support, and downloads them, from GitHub.</span></li>
        <li><span>
          When you add Steam or the Epic Games Store, or install a Windows component, Harbor downloads it from
          its maker: Valve, Epic Games or Microsoft.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          These are plain downloads. They carry nothing about you or your programs, but like any download they
          show the server your internet address. Windows programs you run make their own connections; Harbor
          has no part in those.
        </span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="release release--words" id="statistics">
      <div class="release__head"><h2>Statistics</h2></div>
      <ul>
        <li><span>
          Off until you agree. Harbor asks once, and Harbor &gt; Settings &gt; Privacy has the switch afterwards.
          While it is off nothing is counted and nothing is sent.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          When it is on, Harbor sends one record a day at most, and only on days a program ended. The record
          holds: Harbor’s version, the version of its Windows support and graphics layers, the macOS version
          (such as 27.3), the name of the Mac’s chip (such as Apple M3 Pro), and how many programs ran and how
          many didn’t since the last record, counted by cause: a missing library, .NET, the shader compiler,
          anti-cheat, a crash, or an unexplained early exit.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          It holds no program names, no file names, no account, and no number that tells your Mac from another
          or links one day’s record to the next. Settings shows the next record exactly as it will be sent.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          Records are kept with the day they arrived, without the time, and deleted after 13 months. Because a
          record can’t be traced to anyone, a single one can’t be picked out and deleted on request; switching
          statistics off stops them and discards what was counted but not yet sent.
        </span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="release release--words" id="reports">
      <div class="release__head"><h2>Problem reports</h2></div>
      <ul>
        <li><span>
          A report is only made when you choose Report Problem. Harbor shows all of it first and you can change
          every word. It holds the versions listed above, the cause Harbor found, and the last lines of the
          program’s log, with your user name and your Mac’s name replaced. The log can still mention the program.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          Open in Browser puts the text into a new issue on GitHub, which you submit yourself under your GitHub
          account. Issues are public, and GitHub’s own privacy statement applies.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          Send to Harbor sends the title and the text, and nothing else, to Harbor’s own service. It is kept
          with the day it arrived and deleted after 90 days. Harbor shows a reference for it once; nothing else
          connects the report to you.
        </span></li>
        <li>
          <span>To delete a report sooner, enter its reference here.</span>
          <form class="forget" id="forget">
            <label for="reference">Reference</label>
            <input id="reference" name="reference" required autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="ABCD-EFGH-JKLM-NPQR" />
            <button class="btn" type="submit">Delete report</button>
            <p role="status" id="forget-result"></p>
          </form>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="release release--words" id="where">
      <div class="release__head"><h2>Where it is kept</h2></div>
      <ul>
        <li><span>
          Statistics and reports sent to Harbor are stored in a database in Frankfurt, Germany, hosted by
          Supabase. Only Harbor’s maintainer can read it. Nothing is sold, shared or used for anything but
          finding and fixing problems in Harbor.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          Harbor’s service stores no internet address and no time of day. The hosting company’s own access log
          does record each request’s address, rough location and time, as any web server does, and keeps it
          for about a day. Harbor doesn’t copy it or join it to a record.
        </span></li>
        <li><span>
          Questions, or anything here that doesn’t match what the app does: <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>open an
          issue on GitHub</a>. The service’s code is in the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/service`}>repository</a>.
        </span></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script is:inline define:vars={{ SERVICE }}>
    const form = document.getElementById("forget");
    const result = document.getElementById("forget-result");
    form.addEventListener("submit", async (event) => {
      event.preventDefault();
      const reference = form.reference.value.trim().toUpperCase();
      result.textContent = "Deleting…";
      try {
        const answer = await fetch(`${SERVICE}/reports/${encodeURIComponent(reference)}`, { method: "DELETE" });
        result.textContent = answer.ok ? "Deleted."
          : answer.status === 404 || answer.status === 400 ? "No report has this reference. It may already be deleted."
          : "That didn’t work. Try again later.";
        if (answer.ok) form.reset();
      } catch {
        result.textContent = "The service couldn’t be reached. Try again later.";
      }
    });
  </script>
</Base>
